= Solution
{parent=a}

The Darboux–Riemann criterion says that a <bounded function> on $[a,b]$ is Riemann integrable exactly when, for every $\varepsilon>0$, some partition $P$ satisfies $U(f,P)-L(f,P)<\varepsilon$. Indeed, every lower sum is at most every upper sum. Taking the supremum of lower sums and infimum of upper sums, the criterion makes their difference smaller than every positive $\varepsilon$, so they are equal; this common value is the Riemann <integral>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= b
{parent=4e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Given $\varepsilon>0$, choose a partition of $[a,d]$ whose upper-minus-lower sum is below $\varepsilon$, and refine it by inserting $b,c$. The contribution from subintervals lying in $[b,c]$ is nonnegative and no larger than the total difference. Restricting the refined partition to $[b,c]$ therefore gives upper-minus-lower sum below $\varepsilon$, so the criterion proves integrability there.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

